<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">Question 4 Lecture 8</Text-field></Title>
<Group labelreference="L794" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Use Maple to make the following picture. [The 13 stars in the original flag of the United States.]</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L791" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">                                                   <Plot height="142.0" type="two-dimensional" width="154.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false"></Plot></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L793" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"><Font style="Text" bold="true">Hints:</Font>: Test each step by plotting the points using <Font style="Text" bold="true">style = point</Font> and or <Font style="Text" bold="true">style = line</Font> before continuing to the next step.

Step 1. The following procedure will produce a list of n equally spaced points on a circle of radius r centered at the origin :</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L792" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">   <Font style="Text" bold="true">&gt;with(plots):
     CirclePoints:=proc(n,r) </Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Text" bold="true" layout="Normal"><Font bold="true">         evalf([seq(r*[sin(i*(2*Pi/n)), cos(i*(2*Pi/n))], i=0..n)]); 
     end proc:</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L796" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal259" opaque="false" subscript="false" underline="false" superscript="false" bold="false" foreground="[0,0,0]" italic="false" executable="false" selection-placeholder="false" size="12" readonly="false" background="[255,255,255]" placeholder="false" family="Times New Roman" font_style_name="Normal259" layout="Normal259" linespacing="0.0" initial="0" firstindent="0" leftmargin="0" layout_style_name="Normal259" pagebreak-before="false" spaceabove="0" linebreak="space" rightmargin="0" bulletsuffix="" alignment="left" bullet="none" spacebelow="0">  &gt;plot(CirclePoints(5,1),style = point, symbol = circle, axes = none, color = black):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L795" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Step 2. Use the above procedure to make a list <Font style="Text" bold="true">L</Font> of 5 points on the unit circle. Then you may form a new list <Font style="Text" bold="true">W:=[L[1], L[3], ...,]</Font> which when plotted with the option <Font style="Text" bold="true">style = line</Font> will produce a star. Number the points in the original circle and by hand trace out the star. This will show you how to complete the list for W. </Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Step 3. The following procedure can be used to translate the star (given by <Font style="Text" bold="true">W</Font>) from the origin to a new point <Font style="Text" bold="true">P</Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">   <Font style="Text" bold="true">&gt;translate:=proc(W,P)
        map(x-&gt;x+P,W);
     end proc:</Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"> </Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Step 4. Now create the list , say, <Font style="Text" bold="true">X</Font> of 13 points in a suitably sized circle. For each point <Font style="Text" bold="true">P = X[i]</Font> in the list translate the star <Font style="Text" bold="true">W</Font> to the new point <Font style="Text" bold="true">P</Font> and create a plot of this star. Call it, say,  <Font style="Text" bold="true">Plot[i]</Font>. Do this using a do loop. Finally use <Font style="Text" bold="true">display</Font> to show all these plots at the same time to create the above picture.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
